Step 2 — The script
A few design notes before the code:
hello.txt → 640 = owner rw-, group r--, others --- (4+2+0 / 4+0+0 / 0+0+0).
report.log → owner rwx, group r-x, others none = 750 (4+2+1 / 4+0+1 / 0+0+0).
Date/branch must be dynamic, not hard-coded — pull them live with date and git branch --show-current when the script runs, not typed in as fixed text.
The task says "create files/dirs in this order," so the script follows that order literally, then permissions, then content — matching the exact sequence listed.
#!/bin/bash

# 1. Create the directory structure
mkdir -p my_app/src
mkdir -p my_app/logs

# 2. Create hello.txt (empty)
touch my_app/src/hello.txt

# 3. Create report.log
touch my_app/logs/report.log

# 4. Set permissions
chmod 640 my_app/src/hello.txt
chmod 750 my_app/logs/report.log

# 5. Get dynamic values
CURRENT_DATE=$(date)
CURRENT_BRANCH=$(git branch --show-current)

# 6. Write content to report.log
{
  echo "Project Initialized by: [Full Name]"
  echo ""
  echo "Setup completed on: $CURRENT_DATE"
  echo "Branch name: $CURRENT_BRANCH"
} > my_app/logs/report.log

Replace Full Name with actual name. Save (Ctrl+X, Y, Enter).

One thing to watch: git branch --show-current only works correctly if this script is run from inside the git repo (so it can detect which branch you're on) — running it from question-2/ inside your cloned repo satisfies that automatically.

